WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:02.460
Packaging design is one of my favorite things
to create.

00:00:02.460 --> 00:00:06.810
It has so much space for creativity, it looks
great on your portfolio and if you know what

00:00:06.810 --> 00:00:09.980
you’re doing, companies are willing to pay
good money for it.

00:00:09.980 --> 00:00:13.440
Not to mention you can brag to your friends
when you see the product on the supermarket

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:14.440
shelf.

00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:18.580
So today I’ll give you a sneak peak into
my packaging design process inside Illustrator,

00:00:18.580 --> 00:00:22.740
from dielines and content distribution all
the way to concept, references and all the

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:27.180
stuff that goes through my mind while I’m
choosing colors, elements, fonts and everything

00:00:27.180 --> 00:00:28.180
in between.

00:00:28.180 --> 00:00:29.180
So let’s go!

00:00:29.180 --> 00:00:33.530
Before we can start creating in Illustrator,
we need a few things, the first and most important

00:00:33.530 --> 00:00:35.940
being the briefing of the product itself.

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:39.610
This is something the company you work for
will provide you, but since there’s no company

00:00:39.610 --> 00:00:43.160
on my case, I made it all up myself with the
help of ChatGPT.

00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:48.690
The product will be a single origin, organic
Colombian coffee called Sol y Oro.

00:00:48.690 --> 00:00:53.050
Sol, meaning sun, represents the bright sunlight
that shines over the Andean peaks where the

00:00:53.050 --> 00:00:58.070
coffee is cultivated, and Oro, meaning gold,
is a reference to the famous pre-Columbian

00:00:58.070 --> 00:01:01.450
works of gold made by the indigenous people
of Colombia.

00:01:01.450 --> 00:01:06.280
ChatGPT also gave me a few directions for
the packaging design itself, but nothing that

00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:09.729
an actual brand wouldn’t provide me in a
real scenario.

00:01:09.729 --> 00:01:13.751
With that out of the way, we can start working
on the packaging itself, and for that, we

00:01:13.751 --> 00:01:18.640
need a dieline, which is a template that indicates
the size of the package as well as where the

00:02:24.450 --> 00:02:29.170
of the size and area I have to work with,
I’m gonna start thinking about concept,

00:02:29.170 --> 00:02:32.740
colors, fonts, elements and bringing in references
inside illustrator.

00:02:32.740 --> 00:02:33.740
The first thing I’ll think about is the
general concept of the packaging based on

00:02:33.740 --> 00:02:34.740
our brief.

00:02:34.740 --> 00:02:35.740
With that out of the way, I can think of which
elements I’ll use to bring the concept to

00:02:35.740 --> 00:02:36.740
life, which in turn will naturally lead to
color and font choices.

00:02:36.740 --> 00:02:37.740
If that sounds confusing, come with me, that’s
actually a very simple process.

00:02:37.740 --> 00:02:38.740
My first step for coming up with a concept
is analyzing the name of the brand itself,

00:02:38.740 --> 00:02:43.680
Sol y Oro, which already gives me 2 clues
to start building the concept, which are sun

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:44.849
and gold.

00:02:44.849 --> 00:02:49.300
The briefing said the sun is a reference to
the bright sunlight that hits the Andean peaks

00:02:49.300 --> 00:02:53.940
where the coffee is cultivated and gold references
the gold art made by the indigenous people

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:59.610
of Colombia - that’s at least 3 more clues:
the Andean peaks, gold art and indigenous

00:02:59.610 --> 00:03:00.610
people.

00:03:00.610 --> 00:03:03.020
And I feel like that’s enough for me to
start working.

00:03:03.020 --> 00:03:07.360
Now, I’ll start looking for references,
which will both help me later in creating

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:11.739
the design itself, but also help me tying
all these loose ideas together to create an

00:03:11.739 --> 00:03:13.400
actual concept.

00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:18.040
I feel like sun and mountains give off a natural
sunset beyond the peaks vibe, so I did a little

00:03:18.040 --> 00:03:22.989
research on the Andes and the coffee regions
of Colombia and found out that both regions

00:03:22.989 --> 00:03:28.230
overlap precisely, meaning most of the coffee
in Colombia is cultivated in the mountains,

00:03:28.230 --> 00:03:31.900
which means that the Andean peaks is a safe
reference for the packaging design.

00:03:31.900 --> 00:03:36.569
Remember, I’m working off of ChatGPT information
here, so I’ll fact check everything.

00:03:36.569 --> 00:03:41.379
Then, I researched about the different indigenous
people of Colombia and the pre-Columbian gold

00:03:41.379 --> 00:03:47.099
art, which lead me to the Museo Del Oro in
Bogotá, meaning Museum of Gold, confirming

00:03:47.099 --> 00:03:49.099
the whole gold art story.

00:03:49.099 --> 00:03:53.010
There was a lot of pictures of gold artwork
from the museum, and I brought some of them

00:03:53.010 --> 00:03:58.140
inside Illustrator, but one statue in particular
was drawing my attention and giving me some

00:03:58.140 --> 00:04:00.140
ideas, so I gave it some emphasis.

00:04:00.140 --> 00:04:05.829
I also looked for some references of other
indigenous artwork like clothing and paintings,

00:04:05.829 --> 00:04:09.900
especially from the Wayuu people, because
they have some beautiful design patterns that

00:04:09.900 --> 00:04:13.150
might work very well on the packaging design.

00:04:13.150 --> 00:04:17.739
Last but not least, we also need design references
to have a general understanding of how this

00:04:17.739 --> 00:04:19.739
particular product is designed.

00:04:19.739 --> 00:04:23.830
This will help us both stand out from the
competition, while also staying recognizable

00:04:23.830 --> 00:04:26.050
within our market niche.

00:04:26.050 --> 00:04:29.710
So here’s the whole concept tied together:
the bottom half of the box will be layers

00:04:29.710 --> 00:04:34.770
of mountains in shades of brown, orange and
yellow, with the I sun rising on the horizon.

00:04:34.770 --> 00:04:39.110
I’m giving a lot of emphasis to bright,
warm colors because they’re the main motif

00:04:39.110 --> 00:04:42.780
of this package, both sun and gold are bright
and warm.

00:04:42.780 --> 00:04:46.820
I’m thinking of making the upper half of
the package a deep blue, both to give the

00:04:46.820 --> 00:04:51.860
packaging a lot of contrast with the bright
colors below, but also to represent that early

00:04:51.860 --> 00:04:55.980
evening sky when the sun is about to disappear
on the horizon.

00:04:55.980 --> 00:05:00.440
The blue background will also provide a great
canvas for the shiny yellow logo that I’m

00:05:00.440 --> 00:05:02.440
going to create for Sol y Oro.

00:05:02.440 --> 00:05:07.199
The gold will also be represented in the sky
with a vectorized pictogram of the golden

00:05:07.199 --> 00:05:12.460
statue that I showed before, almost like a
golden god watching over the horizon.

00:05:12.460 --> 00:05:17.610
Lastly, I’ll try to incorporate some of
the Wayuu patterns as details here and there,

00:05:17.610 --> 00:05:21.569
if I feel there is space for it - I don’t
want to make the packaging too overcrowded

00:05:21.569 --> 00:05:23.590
with symbols and elements.

00:05:23.590 --> 00:05:24.590
Alright!

00:05:24.590 --> 00:05:25.590
Now it’s time to work.

00:05:25.590 --> 00:05:27.660
I’ll start by making the mountain colors.

00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:31.500
I want the one further in the back to be a
bright warm yellow and then progressively

00:05:31.500 --> 00:05:35.920
get darker layer after layer until it becomes
a dark reddish brown.

00:05:35.920 --> 00:05:39.880
I want the darker color to be the one on top
because this is the color that’ll extend

00:05:39.880 --> 00:05:43.960
to the entire bottom of the box, leaving a
nice, dark background that I can place information

00:05:43.960 --> 00:05:45.000
on top.

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:48.729
This might seem counterintuitive, since I
said that I want to give emphasis to the bright

00:05:48.729 --> 00:05:53.650
colors, but it’s precisely the presence
of darker colors that will draw even more

00:05:53.650 --> 00:05:56.069
attention to the bright ones.

00:05:56.069 --> 00:05:59.479
Contrast is one of my favorite principles
in the design.

00:05:59.479 --> 00:06:02.419
The same idea of contrast will be used for
the sky.

00:06:02.419 --> 00:06:07.331
I’ll use a dark blue color, which even thought
will make the package darker overall, will

00:06:07.331 --> 00:06:11.270
draw that much more attention to the bright
yellow logo later on.

00:06:11.270 --> 00:06:14.880
Now that I have an idea for the colors, I
can work on the mountains.

00:06:14.880 --> 00:06:18.830
I’ll use the Paintbrush Tool to draw some
random mountain peaks, and if I don’t like

00:06:18.830 --> 00:06:23.020
the result I’ll just delete and draw again
until I get something I like.

00:06:23.020 --> 00:06:27.090
Then I’ll use the Pen Tool to join both
ends by making a rectangle on the bottom.

00:06:27.090 --> 00:06:31.120
I’ll repeat this process 4 more times, changing
the color with each layer.

00:06:31.120 --> 00:06:35.080
I’ll make sure all layers have the same
width, and use the Warp Tool to adjust the

00:06:35.080 --> 00:06:39.539
mountain peaks where necessary, to make the
composition more interesting.

00:06:39.539 --> 00:06:43.500
Since the mountains will go all the way around
the box, I need both ends to match when the

00:06:43.500 --> 00:06:48.210
box is put together, so I’ll duplicate all
the mountains, place them side by side and

00:06:48.210 --> 00:06:50.919
then edit the right side to match the left.

00:06:50.919 --> 00:06:55.030
I’ll place these mountains on the file with
the dieline, adjust it’s size to match the

00:06:55.030 --> 00:06:59.210
width of the box, and then make two copies,
one on each side.

00:06:59.210 --> 00:07:03.770
I need the drawing to extend to the sides
so it can cover the glue area as well as the

00:07:03.770 --> 00:07:04.770
bleed.

00:07:04.770 --> 00:07:08.979
To fill the rest of the box with brown, I’m
actually going to make a duplicate of the

00:07:08.979 --> 00:07:14.229
bleed markings and paint it with brown, then
just delete the anchor points up top and adjust

00:07:14.229 --> 00:07:15.370
the height.

00:07:15.370 --> 00:07:19.449
For the skies I’m gonna do the exact same
thing, duplicate the bleed markings, paint

00:07:19.449 --> 00:07:22.960
it blue, and then delete the anchor points
on the bottom.

00:07:22.960 --> 00:07:27.139
The base layer of the packaging is done, and
now I want to work on the logo, which will

00:07:27.139 --> 00:07:28.139
be just typographic.

00:07:28.139 --> 00:07:33.370
I browsed Adobe Fonts to see if I could find
something that related to the project somehow,

00:07:33.370 --> 00:07:37.620
and I found this font called Jetlab, which
has a very geometric vibe which is going to

00:07:37.620 --> 00:07:42.400
fit perfectly with the project, it reminded
me a lot of the Wayuu art patterns.

00:07:42.400 --> 00:07:47.460
This font also reminded me of Latin American
ethnic art and design in general, just because

00:07:47.460 --> 00:07:50.760
of how common geometric patterns are in these
designs.

00:07:50.760 --> 00:07:55.960
For the logo I wrote Sol y Oro with the font
I just downloaded, outlined the text and then

00:07:55.960 --> 00:07:59.199
edited the characters so the logo would resemble
a rising sun.

00:07:59.199 --> 00:08:03.620
I believe the amount of detail in the font
and the different sized letters is enough

00:08:03.620 --> 00:08:08.229
for this logo to stand on its own without
the need of a symbol to support it.

00:08:08.229 --> 00:08:13.650
I painted the logo a lighter shade of yellow
and placed it on the front panel of the package.

00:08:13.650 --> 00:08:17.810
For the secondary font I need something that’s
easier to read, since the font I used on the

00:08:17.810 --> 00:08:23.289
logo is not great for legibility, but at the
same time it also has to be bold and geometric

00:08:23.289 --> 00:08:25.069
to match the rest of the design.

00:08:25.069 --> 00:08:29.620
I think sun and gold are symbols of power
and strength, so I need the elements of the

00:08:29.620 --> 00:08:31.120
design to relate to that.

00:08:31.120 --> 00:08:37.130
So, there’s no better choice than probably
the most beloved free font among all designers:

00:08:37.130 --> 00:08:38.240
Montserrat.

00:08:38.240 --> 00:08:41.690
Below the logo I’m gonna write Colombian
Coffee and then fill the rest of the length

00:08:41.690 --> 00:08:43.770
of the logo with two white lines.

00:08:43.770 --> 00:08:48.690
I’ll use the same font, size and color to
add other information to the front of the

00:08:48.690 --> 00:08:53.770
packaging, like weight and whole bean at the
bottom and organic and single origin at the

00:08:53.770 --> 00:08:54.770
top.

00:08:54.770 --> 00:08:58.670
There’s still a lot of space on the bottom
of the package, so I’m gonna use it to inform

00:08:58.670 --> 00:09:02.510
some useful information about the coffee - this
is something that I’m bringing in from my

00:09:02.510 --> 00:09:04.020
design reference board.

00:09:04.020 --> 00:09:09.060
I’ll make a 2x3 grid, aligned with the width
if the logo, and inside each cell I’ll place

00:09:09.060 --> 00:09:16.740
a different characteristic of the coffee:
variety, roast, altitude, body and acidity.

00:09:16.740 --> 00:09:21.260
In the remaining cell I’ll place the roasting
date, but since it’ll be manually written,

00:09:21.260 --> 00:09:25.950
I’ll add a white background and use a handwritten
font to simulate it.

00:09:25.950 --> 00:09:30.640
Lastly, I’ll add the established date of
the company at the top of the packaging, aligning

00:09:30.640 --> 00:09:33.600
it to the other elements as necessary.

00:09:33.600 --> 00:09:37.310
Now here’s what I’m planning for the other
3 sides: on the right I’ll add the tasting

00:09:37.310 --> 00:09:42.190
notes of the coffee and information about
acidity, bitterness and body.

00:09:42.190 --> 00:09:46.420
On the left side I’ll add instructions on
how to make the perfect pour over, with things

00:09:46.420 --> 00:09:50.500
like grind size, water temperature and coffee
to water ratio.

00:09:50.500 --> 00:09:54.910
Finally, on the back of the packaging, I think
all add some information about the coffee

00:09:54.910 --> 00:09:59.740
or the brand, like where it was cultivated
and highlighting qualities like organic and

00:09:59.740 --> 00:10:00.740
single origin.

00:10:00.740 --> 00:10:02.860
I don’t know, I’ll think about it.

00:10:02.860 --> 00:10:07.140
It’s worth noting that since I’m not making
this for an actual client, I’m having to

00:10:07.140 --> 00:10:11.040
come up with what information I’ll add to
the package and where I’ll add them.

00:10:11.040 --> 00:10:15.130
I’m using other coffee packages as reference,
but again, this is something that the company

00:10:15.130 --> 00:10:16.450
would inform you.

00:10:16.450 --> 00:10:18.520
Ok, let’s move on.

00:10:18.520 --> 00:10:22.880
Starting with the right side, I used the same
font I used for the logo to write the title,

00:10:22.880 --> 00:10:27.540
using the front of the package as a reference
for alignment, and then just added a few tasting

00:10:27.540 --> 00:10:32.450
notes that I thought make sense for this coffee,
but again, if it was a real project, that’s

00:10:32.450 --> 00:10:34.120
something the brand would inform me.

00:10:34.120 --> 00:10:39.899
Below, I used these circles to create a scale
of 1 to 5 for acidity, bitterness and body

00:10:39.899 --> 00:10:41.279
of the coffee.

00:10:41.279 --> 00:10:45.740
On the left side I used the same font and
alignment for the title, and added a few instruction

00:10:45.740 --> 00:10:48.769
on how to get the best results out of your
pour over.

00:10:48.769 --> 00:10:52.899
I designed the icons myself because they were
very simple, but you can easily find similar

00:10:52.899 --> 00:10:55.320
icons for free on the internet.

00:10:55.320 --> 00:11:00.220
Below the instructions I used the remaining
space to add the barcode of the product.

00:11:00.220 --> 00:11:04.680
On the back of the package I added the title
“hot as the sun, precious as gold” and

00:11:04.680 --> 00:11:06.440
that’s as far as I’ve made it.

00:11:06.440 --> 00:11:11.240
At this point, I was empty on creativity to
keep coming up with coffee things, so I kindly

00:11:11.240 --> 00:11:14.980
asked ChatGPT to write me a paragraph about
the product.

00:11:14.980 --> 00:11:18.680
And I must say, the end result was pretty
awesome.

00:11:18.680 --> 00:11:25.880
"Nestled among the high Andean peaks, Sol
y Oro emerges as a Colombian coffee of pure

00:11:25.880 --> 00:11:26.880
distinction.

00:11:26.880 --> 00:11:31.269
Cultivated organically in the crisp mountain
air, its beans carry a subtle harmony, nurtured

00:11:31.269 --> 00:11:33.800
by the sun's golden touch.

00:11:33.800 --> 00:11:39.860
With low acidity and a satisfying body, each
sip of Sol y Oro unveils a delightful balance.

00:11:39.860 --> 00:11:44.480
From the verdant hills of the mountains, this
single origin treasure brings forth flavors

00:11:44.480 --> 00:11:47.450
that ground the senses and elevate the everyday.”

00:11:47.450 --> 00:11:52.410
Hahahahahaha alright.

00:11:52.410 --> 00:11:56.200
The text was looking great, but this back
part of the packaging was still looking kinda

00:11:56.200 --> 00:11:59.779
simple and empty, so I decided to add some
more detail.

00:11:59.779 --> 00:12:04.639
I started by adding the rising sun, as I had
previously planned early on, which just consists

00:12:04.639 --> 00:12:08.890
in a single yellow circle behind the mountains,
so it was still not enough.

00:12:08.890 --> 00:12:14.040
However, I remembered that I had some pictures
of Wayuu art saved in my reference board,

00:12:14.040 --> 00:12:18.820
and decided to use those to create some kind
of pattern that I could use as a frame around

00:12:18.820 --> 00:12:21.190
the text, so that’s what I did.

00:12:21.190 --> 00:12:26.430
As I said before, a lot of Latin American
ethnic art revolves around geometric shapes,

00:12:26.430 --> 00:12:31.980
so coming up with a pattern was not a super
hard task, and it was also really fun to do.

00:12:31.980 --> 00:12:36.111
Once I had the pattern ready, I arranged it
into a frame around the text and used the

00:12:36.111 --> 00:12:40.720
gradient panel to add a fade to the pattern
as it dips behind the mountains, and I think

00:12:40.720 --> 00:12:42.930
this looks really, really cool.

00:12:42.930 --> 00:12:47.199
Now the last major element that was missing
was the gold statue, so I started vectorizing

00:12:47.199 --> 00:12:48.199
it.

00:12:48.199 --> 00:12:52.480
What I thought would be a quick thing to do
ended up taking quite a while, vectorizing

00:12:52.480 --> 00:12:55.450
is usually an easy task, but not this one.

00:12:55.450 --> 00:13:00.190
The statue was entirely made of gold and sometimes
it was hard to understand its shape.

00:13:00.190 --> 00:13:04.490
But with a few different reference pictures,
I managed to get to a result that I was happy

00:13:04.490 --> 00:13:05.490
with.

00:13:05.490 --> 00:13:10.010
Once I was done, I brought the statue vector
over to the main file, and used it as a very

00:13:10.010 --> 00:13:15.040
subtle background right in the front of the
package, and I loved the result.

00:13:15.040 --> 00:13:20.800
It looked like a god or a warrior watching
over the land, which reinforced the connotation

00:13:20.800 --> 00:13:24.210
of power and strength that sun and gold carry.

00:13:24.210 --> 00:13:28.930
Now, all that’s left to do is add a few
details here and there, just to tie everything

00:13:28.930 --> 00:13:29.930
together.

00:13:29.930 --> 00:13:34.699
I painted the dust flaps yellow and added
two pictograms: one representing the sun and

00:13:34.699 --> 00:13:36.560
the other representing the gold.

00:13:36.560 --> 00:13:41.170
The gold one is a simplified version of the
golden statue, now with a full body instead

00:13:41.170 --> 00:13:42.410
of just the head.

00:13:42.410 --> 00:13:46.510
Both pictograms use just a slightly darker
shade of yellow, because I don’t want them

00:13:46.510 --> 00:13:48.040
to draw too much attention.

00:13:48.040 --> 00:13:52.300
It’s just a fun little surprise for the
customer as they open the box.

00:13:52.300 --> 00:13:56.050
I also painted the tuck in flap yellow as
well.

00:13:56.050 --> 00:13:59.769
The bottom half of the package was feeling
a little bit empty, so I added some elements

00:13:59.769 --> 00:14:00.870
there as well.

00:14:00.870 --> 00:14:05.500
On the sides, I added once again the head
of the golden statue, and just like the pictograms

00:14:05.500 --> 00:14:10.570
on the dust flaps, I colored them with a very
slightly brighter shade of the brown, because

00:14:10.570 --> 00:14:14.200
they are not the focus of the design, they’re
just a detail.

00:14:14.200 --> 00:14:19.160
On the back of the package, I place the logo
of Sol y Oro, using the same color.

00:14:19.160 --> 00:14:24.050
On the top panel of the package I placed once
again the logo in its original bright yellow,

00:14:24.050 --> 00:14:28.190
and used the same pattern that I used on the
back of the package, but this time in a darker

00:14:28.190 --> 00:14:31.610
shade of blue, creating a frame all around
the logo.

00:14:31.610 --> 00:14:36.300
It’s important to be aware that the logo
should be placed upside down on the top panel,

00:14:36.300 --> 00:14:39.700
so that it is not upside down when the package
is closed.

00:14:39.700 --> 00:14:43.029
My final touch was to add a few glows here
and there.

00:14:43.029 --> 00:14:46.790
Since both the sun and gold are shiny things,
I thought that would look nice.

00:14:46.790 --> 00:14:51.190
I added a glow all throughout the mountain
range, another glow on the big sun on the

00:14:51.190 --> 00:14:55.019
back of the package and lastly on the logo
on the top panel.

00:17:22.250 --> 00:17:24.620
If you enjoyed this video, check this other
one here.

00:17:24.620 --> 00:17:29.060
A special thanks to all my Patreon supporters
who help me make high quality content like

00:17:29.060 --> 00:17:30.060
this one.

